WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.749
Welcome to Module Ten: Safety, Ethics, and Compliance.

2
00:00:04.079 --> 00:00:08.210
Alignment makes a model usually behave; this module makes sure it does.

3
00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:13.845
Picture a travel company's booking assistant that reads web pages and calls tools.

4
00:00:14.175 --> 00:00:16.301
In its first week, three things go wrong.

5
00:00:17.101 --> 00:00:21.297
First, a user role-plays the bot into writing content it should refuse.

6
00:00:21.627 --> 00:00:24.969
That's a jailbreak: attacking the model's own safety policy.

7
00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:30.711
Second, a hotel page hides white text telling the bot to leak customer details.

8
00:00:31.041 --> 00:00:34.980
That's indirect prompt injection, and the user was completely innocent.

9
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.823
Third, a customer's passport number turns up in the logs.

10
00:00:39.153 --> 00:00:41.599
Nobody meant to store it, and nothing stopped it.

11
00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:47.085
A system-prompt line saying "ignore injected instructions" fixes none of this.

12
00:00:47.415 --> 00:00:51.760
The fix is architecture: guardrails that check what goes in and what comes out.

13
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.987
Lesson one builds those guardrails with NeMo Guardrails.

14
00:00:56.317 --> 00:01:01.643
Input, dialog, retrieval, execution and output rails each catch different failures.

15
00:01:02.443 --> 00:01:07.833
Safety classifiers like Llama Guard and NemoGuard label each turn safe or unsafe.

16
00:01:08.163 --> 00:01:12.743
Colang flows keep conversations on topic, and tool calls need allow-lists.

17
00:01:13.543 --> 00:01:17.695
PII is masked before it reaches the model, the answer or the logs.

18
00:01:18.025 --> 00:01:22.178
And because every rail adds latency, you'll layer defences that stay fast.

19
00:01:22.978 --> 00:01:26.320
Lesson two turns to responsible AI, starting with bias.

20
00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:31.763
Swap only the name on identical CVs, and see whether the hiring advice changes.

21
00:01:32.563 --> 00:01:33.927
Counterfactual tests,

22
00:01:33.977 --> 00:01:38.507
fairness metrics and benchmarks like BBQ turn a worry into a number.

23
00:01:38.837 --> 00:01:42.499
Mitigations then span data, training, inference and monitoring.

24
00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:47.409
Next comes red teaming: attacking your own system before anyone else does.

25
00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:52.660
Scanners like garak fire thousands of probes, and humans find the creative failures.

26
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.317
Every model ships with a model card: intended use, limits, and results by group.

27
00:01:58.647 --> 00:02:02.053
And licenses follow the weights and data into your fine-tune.

28
00:02:02.853 --> 00:02:05.512
Regulation depends on how a model is used.

29
00:02:05.842 --> 00:02:09.380
Under the EU AI Act, a writing helper is limited risk,

30
00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:12.171
but screening job applicants is high risk.

31
00:02:12.971 --> 00:02:15.471
So compliance is monitored continuously:

32
00:02:15.521 --> 00:02:18.595
guardrail blocks, refusals and outcomes by group.

33
00:02:18.925 --> 00:02:21.819
And every model or prompt change triggers a fresh audit.

34
00:02:22.619 --> 00:02:25.449
By the end, you'll wrap an LLM in guardrails,

35
00:02:25.499 --> 00:02:28.521
audit it for bias, red-team it, and document it.

36
00:02:28.851 --> 00:02:31.127
Let's start with lesson one: guardrails.
